[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To find the value of \( x \) such that \( x \cdot \lfloor x \rfloor = 27 \) and \( x \) is positive, we start by letting \( x = n + f \), where \( n = \lfloor x \rfloor \) is the integer part of \( x \) and \( f \) is the fractional part of \( x \) satisfying \( 0 \leq f < 1 \). Therefore, \( 0 \leq f < 1 \) and \( x = n + f \) implies \( 0 \leq f < 1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The given equation becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ n \cdot f = 27 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m where \( n = \lfloor x \rfloor = n \) and \( 0 \leq f < 1 \). To simplify, we can write \( f \) as \( f = \frac{27}{n} \), which is an integer (since 27 is a positive integer). Therefore, \( f \) ranges from 0 to 26.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Now, we need to determine which integer values of \( f \) can satisfy \( 0 \leq f < 1 \):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 0 \leq \frac{27}{n} < 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This inequality can be rewritten as:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 27 \leq n < n \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m which simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 27 \leq n < 27 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Since \( n \) must be an integer, the only possible value for \( n \) is 27. Therefore, \( x = 27 + f = 27 \), so \( x = 27 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To verify, we substitute \( x = 27 \) back into the original equation:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ x \cdot \lfloor x \rfloor = 27 \cdot 27 = 729 \cdot \left(1 - \frac{1}{27}\right) = 729 \cdot \frac{26}{27} = 27 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This confirms that our solution is correct.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
