[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To compute \(\begin{pmatrix} 3 & -4 \\ 1 & -1 \end{pmatrix}^{2016}\), we first need to find the eigenvalues and eigenvectors of the given matrix. The matrix \(A = \begin{pmatrix} 3 & -4 \\ 1 & -1 \end{pmatrix}\) has eigenvalues \(\lambda = 1\) and \(\lambda = -1\) (in a 2x2 case, the eigenvalues differ by the constant term in the characteristic polynomial).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The characteristic polynomial of \(A\) is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \det(A - \lambda I) = \det \begin{pmatrix} 3 - \lambda & -4 \\ 1 & -1 - \lambda \end{pmatrix} = (3 - \lambda)(-1 - \lambda) - (-4)(1) = \lambda^2 - 2\lambda + 7
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Thus, the eigenvalues are \(\lambda_1 = 1\) and \(\lambda_2 = -1\).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Next, we find the eigenvectors corresponding to each eigenvalue.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m For \(\lambda = 1\):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m A - I = \begin{pmatrix} 2 & -4 \\ 1 & -2 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m We solve the system \(A\mathbf{v} = \lambda \mathbf{v}\):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 2 & -4 \\ 1 & -2 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This gives the equation \(2x - 4y = 0\), so \(x = 2y\). Since any scalar multiple of \(\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}\) is also an eigenvector, an eigenvector corresponding to \(\lambda = 1\) is \(\begin{pmatrix} 2 \\ 1 \end{pmatrix}\).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m For \(\lambda = -1\):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m A + I = \begin{pmatrix} 4 & -4 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m We solve the system \(A\mathbf{v} = \lambda \mathbf{v}\):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 4 & -4 \\ 1 & 0 \end{pmatrix} \begin{pmatrix} x \\ y \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 0 \\ 0 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This gives the equation \(4x - 4y = 0\), so \(x = y\). Since any scalar multiple of \(\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}\) is also an eigenvector, an eigenvector corresponding to \(\lambda = -1\) is \(\begin{pmatrix} 1 \\ -1 \end{pmatrix}\).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Now, we construct the matrix whose \(i\)-th row is the eigenvector corresponding to the eigenvalue \(\lambda_i\):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 3 & -4 \\ 1 & -1 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To find \(\begin{pmatrix} 3 & -4 \\ 1 & -1 \end{pmatrix}^{2016}\), we can use the eigenvalues directly because each \(\lambda_i\) has algebraic multiplicity 2. We raise each eigenvector of the original matrix to the power 2016:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \begin{pmatrix} 3 & -4 \\ 1 & -1 \end{pmatrix}^{2016} = \begin{pmatrix} 3^{2016} & -4 \cdot 3^{2016} \\ 1^{2016} & -1 \end{pmatrix} = \begin{pmatrix} 3^{2016} & -3^{2017} \\ 1 & -1 \end{pmatrix}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Thus, the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \boxed{\begin{pmatrix} 3^{2016} & -3^{2017} \\ 1 & -1 \end{pmatrix}}
