[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ y = 2k^2 (2 \sin \theta \cos \theta) = 2k^2 \sqrt{2} \sin \theta \cos \theta. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Since \( \sin \theta \cos \theta = \frac{1}{2} \sin 2\theta \), we can rewrite the expression for \( y \) as:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ y = 2k^2 \sqrt{2} \frac{1}{2} \sin 2\theta = k^2 \sqrt{2} \sin 2\theta. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The maximum value of \( \sin 2\theta \) is 1, so the maximum value of \( y \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ y_{\text{max}} = k^2 \sqrt{2} \cdot 1 = k^2 \sqrt{2}. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This maximum value occurs when \( \sin 2\theta = 1 \) or \( \sin 2\theta = -1 \). In these cases, \( k^2 = 1 \) and:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ k = \pm 1 \implies \sin 2\theta = 1 \implies 2 \sin \theta \cos \theta = 1 \implies \sin \theta \cos \theta = \frac{1}{2}. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The maximum value of \( \sin \theta \cos \theta \) is \( \frac{1}{2} \), so the maximum value of \( y^2 \) is:
