[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 13 \times 13 \times 13 = 13^3
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Calculating \(13^3\):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 13^3 = 13 \times 13 \times 13 = 169 \times 13 = 2197
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m So, there are 2197 different ways to draw three hearts.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Next, we determine the number of ways to draw three hearts from the 13 cards of hearts. Since the order in which we draw the cards does not matter (drawing a heart of club, then a heart of diamonds, etc.), we use the combination formula \(\binom{n}{k}\), which represents the number of ways to choose \(k\) items from \(n\) items without regard to order. Here, we are choosing 3 cards from 13 hearts, so:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \binom{13}{3} = \frac{13!}{3!(13-3)!} = \frac{13 \times 12 \times 11}{3 \times 2 \times 1} = \frac{1716}{6} = 286
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m So, there are 286 different ways to draw three hearts.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The probability of drawing three hearts is the number of favorable outcomes divided by the total number of outcomes. Therefore, the probability is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \frac{286}{2197}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m So, the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \boxed{\frac{286}{2197}}
