[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To determine the remainder when \(1999^{2000}\) is divided by 5, we start by finding the remainder of 1999 when divided by 5. First, we perform the division:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 1999 \div 5 = 399 \text{ remainder } 4. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This tells us that \(1999 \equiv 4 \pmod{5}\). Thus, we can express \(1999^{2000}\) in terms of a power of 5:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 1999^{2000} \equiv (4)^{2000} \pmod{5}. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Next, we need to determine the value of \(4^{2000} \pmod{5}\). We observe that the powers of 4 modulo 5 repeat every 4 terms: \(4^1 \equiv 4 \pmod{5}\), \(4^2 \equiv 1 \pmod{5}\), and \(4^3 \equiv 4 \pmod{5}\), \(4^4 \equiv 1 \pmod{5}\), and so on. This pattern indicates that \(4^4 \equiv 1 \pmod{5}\). Therefore, we can reduce the exponent modulo 4:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 2000 \div 4 = 500 \text{ remainder } 0. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This tells us that \(2000 \equiv 0 \pmod{4}\). Thus, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 4^{2000} \equiv 4^4 \equiv 1 \pmod{5}. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m So, the remainder when \(1999^{2000}\) is divided by 5 is \(\boxed{1}\).<|im_end|>
