[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To find the quadratic polynomial \( p(x) = ax^2 + bx + c \) that satisfies the conditions \( p(-3) = 10 \), \( p(0) = 1 \), and \( p(2) = 5 \), we will substitute these points into the polynomial and solve the resulting system of equations.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m First, substitute \( x = -3 \) into the polynomial:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m p(-3) = a(-3)^2 + b(-3) + c = 9a - 3b + c = 10.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This gives us the first equation:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 9a - 3b + c = 10. \quad \text{(1)}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Next, substitute \( x = 0 \) into the polynomial:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m p(0) = a(0)^2 + b(0) + c = c = 1.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m So, we have \( c = 1 \). Substitute this into equation (1):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 9a - 3b + 1 = 10 \implies 9a - 3b = 9 \implies 3a - b = 3. \quad \text{(2)}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Then substitute \( x = 2 \) into the polynomial:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m p(2) = a(2)^2 + b(2) + c = 4a + 2b + 1 = 5.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This gives us the second equation:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 4a + 2b + 1 = 5 \implies 4a + 2b = 4 \implies 2a + b = 2. \quad \text{(3)}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Now we have the system of linear equations:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \begin{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 3a - b = 3, \\
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 2a + b = 2.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \end{cases}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Add the two equations to eliminate \( b \):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m (3a - b) + (2a + b) = 3 + 2 \implies 5a = 5 \implies a = 1.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Substitute \( a = 1 \) back into equation (3):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 2(1) + b = 2 \implies 2 + b = 2 \implies b = 0.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Thus, the coefficients \( a \), \( b \), and \( c \) are \( a = 1 \), \( b = 0 \), and \( c = 1 \). The quadratic polynomial is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m p(x) = x^2 + 1.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To verify, we substitute \( x = -3 \), \( x = 0 \), and \( x = 2 \) into \( p(x) = x^2 + 1 \):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m p(-3) = (-3)^2 + 1 = 9 + 1 = 10,
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m p(0) = 0^2 + 1 = 1,
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m p(2) = 2^2 + 1 = 4 + 1 = 5.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m All conditions are met, so the polynomial is correct. The final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
