[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To solve the equation \(\log_9(2x-7) = \dfrac{3}{2}\), we start by converting the logarithmic equation to its exponential form. The equation \(\log_9(2x-7) = \dfrac{3}{2}\) means that \(9^{\dfrac{3}{2}} = 2x-7\).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Next, we use the property of logarithms that \(a^{\log_a b} = b\). Here, \(a = 3\) and \(b = 2\), so we have \(3^{\dfrac{3}{2}} = 2x-7\).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m We know that \(3^{\dfrac{3}{2}} = (3^{\dfrac{1}{2}})^3 = \sqrt{3}^3 = \sqrt{27} = 3\sqrt{3}\). Therefore, the equation \(3^{\dfrac{3}{2}} = 2x-7\) becomes \(3\sqrt{3} = 2x-7\).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To solve for \(x\), we first isolate the term involving \(x\) by adding 7 to both sides of the equation:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[3\sqrt{3} + 7 = 2x.\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Next, we divide both sides by 2:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[\frac{3\sqrt{3} + 7}{2} = x.\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Thus, the value of \(x\) is \(\boxed{\frac{3\sqrt{3} + 7}{2}}\).<|im_end|>
