[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To determine the probability that a point \( P \) randomly selected from the square region with vertices at \((\pm 2, \pm 2)\) is within one unit of the origin, we need to follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 1. **Identify the area of the square:**
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    The side length of the square is \(2 \times 2 = 4\). Therefore, the area of the square is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    \text{Area of the square} = 4 \times 4 = 16
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 2. **Determine the area of the region within one unit of the origin:**
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    The region within one unit of the origin consists of the interior of a circle with radius 1. The area of a circle with radius 1 is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    \pi \times 1^2 = \pi
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 3. **Calculate the probability:**
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    The probability that a randomly selected point is within one unit of the origin is the ratio of the area of the circle to the area of the square. Thus, the probability \( P \) is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    P = \frac{\text{Area of the circle}}{\text{Area of the square}} = \frac{\pi}{16}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Therefore, the probability that a point \( P \) is within one unit of the origin is \(\boxed{\frac{\pi}{16}}\).<|im_end|>
