[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To determine the units digit of \(19^{19} + 99^{99}\), we need to find the units digits of the powers of 19 and the powers of 99 separately and then sum these units digits for each term, focusing only on the units digit of the sum.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m First, let's find the units digit of \(19^{19}\). The units digit of 19 repeats every 10 powers of 19 because \(19 \equiv 9 \pmod{10}\). Therefore, the units digit of \(19^{19}\) is the same as the units digit of \(9^{19}\), because the units digits of the powers of 9 repeat in a cycle of 10. The units digit of \(9^{19}\) is determined by the units digit of \(9^{1}\) repeated 19 times, which is 9 because \(9^1 = 9\). So the units digit of \(19^{19}\) is 9.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Next, let's find the units digit of \(99^{99}\). The units digit of 99 is 9 because \(99 \equiv 9 \pmod{10}\). Any power of 9 repeated many times will have the same units digit as the base. Therefore, the units digit of \(99^{99}\) is the same as the units digit of \(9^9\). We need to find the units digit of \(9^9\). The units digits of the first few powers of 9 are 9, 1, 9, 1, etc. Therefore, the units digit of \(9^9\) is 9.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
