[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of \(6^210^2\) and \(15^4\), we start by expressing the numbers in terms of their prime factorizations.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m First, factorize each number:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m - \(6^210^2\) is a product of \(2\) raised to the power of \(2\) and \(3\) raised to the power of \(1\) (or simply 6 raised to the power of \(210\)). Therefore, \(\gcd(6^210^2, 15^4) = 2^1 \cdot 3^2 \cdot \gcd(6^2, 15^2)\).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Next, we factorize the right-hand side of the equation. Note that \(15 = 3 \cdot 5\), so \(15^2 = 3^2 \cdot 5^2\). We also know \(6 = 2 \cdot 3\), so \(6^2 = (2 \cdot 3)^2 = 2^2 \cdot 3^2\). Thus:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \gcd(6^2, 15^2) = \gcd(2^2 \cdot 3^2, 3^2 \cdot 5^2) = 2^2 \cdot 3^2 = 4 \cdot 9 = 36.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Therefore, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \gcd(6^210^2, 15^4) = 2^1 \cdot 3^2 \cdot 36 = 2^1 \cdot 27^2 = 2^2 \cdot 3^2 \cdot 9^2 = 4 \cdot 3^2 \cdot 9^2 = 36^2.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m So the final answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \boxed{36}.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]<|im_end|>
