[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To find the shortest possible length of the remaining side of the triangle, we start by identifying all sides of the triangle. The sides are 6 cm, 8 cm, and the third side \(x\) which is perpendicular to the sides of lengths 6 cm and 8 cm. The hypotenuse can be found using the Pythagorean theorem:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \sqrt{6^2 + 8^2} = \sqrt{36 + 64} = \sqrt{100} = 10 \text{ cm}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Since the right angle is at the vertex where the 8 cm side meets the hypotenuse, we can use the cosine function to find the third side \(x\):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \cos(\theta) = \frac{6}{10} = 0.6 \implies x = 10 \cos(\theta) = 10 \cdot 0.6 = 6 \text{ cm}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m So, all three sides of the triangle are 6 cm, 8 cm, and 10 cm. Now, we check if these side lengths can form a triangle (we use the triangle inequality theorem, which states that the sum of any two sides must be greater than the third side):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 6 + 8 > 10 \implies 14 > 10 \quad \text{(true)}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
