[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To determine the values of \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\) for the quadratic function \(y = ax^2 + bx + c\), we can use the given graph. From the graph, we see that the vertex of the parabola is at \((1, -2)\).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m A quadratic function in the form \(y = ax^2 + bx + c\) has its vertex at \((- \frac{b}{2a}, f(-\frac{b}{2a}))\). For the vertex at \((1, -2)\), we have:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ -\frac{b}{2a} = 1 \implies b = -2a. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m We also know that the function passes through the point \((0, c)\). Therefore:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ c = a(0)^2 + b(0) + c = c. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Since the parabola passes through \((0, c)\), substituting \(x = 0\) into the equation \(y = ax^2 + bx + c\), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ c = ac + c \implies c - c(a + 1) = 0 \implies c(1 - a - 1) = 0 \implies c = 0. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Thus, \(c = 0\).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Now we have \(b = -2a\) and \(c = 0\). For the vertex form of the quadratic function is given by \(y = a(x-1)^2\), we compare this with the vertex form \(y = a(x - (-\frac{b}{2a}))\). Substituting \(b = -2a\) and \(c = 0\) into the vertex form, we get:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ y = a\left(x - (-1)^2\right) = a\left(x - 1\right) = ax - a. \]
