[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To solve the problem of determining the total number of possible guesses for the prices of prizes A, B, and C, where each prize is worth a whole number between 1 and 9999, and the digits given are 1, 1, 1, 1, 3, 3, 3, we need to carefully analyze the constraints and possibilities.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m First, let's rewrite the digits given as 1, 1, 1, 1, 3, 3, 3. This means that the digit 1 can appear in each of the three remaining slots (individually) in any combination of three out of the four remaining digits. The only restriction is that each digit (1, 1, 3, 3) appears exactly once among the four slots for the three prizes.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The digit 3 appears 4 times, and it can be placed in any one of the four slots. There are \(4!\) (4 factorial) possible arrangements for the four slots such that the digit 3 appears anywhere. Similarly, the digit 1 appears 3 times, and can be placed in any one of the four slots. And the digit 1 appears 3 times, and can be placed in any one of the four slots. The digit 1 can appear in the first, third, or fourth slot.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m So, for the digit 1, there are \(3! = 6\) possible arrangements. The digit 3 can appear in any one of the four slots 4! = 24 times. Similarly, the digit 1 can appear in any one of the four slots 3! = 6 times.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
