[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To determine the values of \( x \) that make the expression \(\frac{x^2-9}{(x^2+2x-3)(x-3)}\) undefined, we need to identify the values of \( x \) that make the denominator zero. The denominator is \((x^2+2x-3)(x-3)\). First, we factorize the quadratic expression in the denominator:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ x^2 + 2x - 3 = (x+3)(x-1). \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m So, the denominator can be factored as:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ (x^2 + 2x - 3)(x - 3) = (x+3)(x-1)(x-3). \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The expression \(\frac{x^2-9}{(x^2+2x-3)(x-3)}\) is undefined when the denominator is zero. Therefore, we set the denominator equal to zero and solve for \( x \):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ (x+3)(x-1)(x-3) = 0. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This equation is satisfied when any of the factors is zero. So, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 1. \( x + 3 = 0 \) which gives \( x = -3 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 2. \( x - 1 = 0 \) which gives \( x = 1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 3. \( x - 3 = 0 \) which gives \( x = 3 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Thus, the values of \( x \) that make the expression undefined are:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ x = -3, \quad x = 1, \quad x = 3. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m There are 3 such values. Therefore, the number of values of \( x \) that make the expression undefined is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \boxed{3}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \]<|im_end|>
