[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To determine the smallest positive integer \( a \) such that \( a^{-1} \) is undefined in both congruences modulo 55 and modulo 66, we need to find the least common multiple (LCM) of the moduli \( 55 \) and \( 66 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m First, factorize the moduli:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 55 = 5 \times 11 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 66 = 2 \times 3 \times 11 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Next, find the highest powers of all prime factors in the factorizations:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m - The prime factor 2 appears in the factorization of both, so the minimum power of 2 used is \( 2^1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m - The prime factor 3 appears in the factorization of \( 66 \), so the minimum power of 3 used is \( 3^1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m - The prime factor 5 appears in the factorization of \( 55 \), so the minimum power of 5 used is \( 5^0 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m - The prime factor 11 appears in the factorization of \( 66 \), so the minimum power of 11 used is \( 11^1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The LCM is thus:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \text{LCM}(55, 66) = 2^1 \times 3^1 \times 5^0 \times 11^1 = 2 \times 3 \times 11 = 66 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Therefore, the smallest positive integer \( a \) such that \( a^{-1} \) is undefined in both congruences is the LCM itself, which is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ a = 66 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Thus, the answer is:
