[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To determine how much the area of the smaller square changes when its length and width are each decreased by 60 units, we can follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 1. Let the original side length of the smaller square be \(3491\) units. The original area \(A_{\text{original}}\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    A_{\text{original}} = 3491 \times 3491
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 2. When the side length is decreased by 60 units, the new side length becomes:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    3491 - 60 = 3431 \text{ units}
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    The new area \(A_{\text{new}}\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    A_{\text{new}} = 3431 \times 3431
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 3. The change in area \(\Delta A\) due to the decrease in both dimensions is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    \[
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    \Delta A = A_{\text{original}} - A_{\text{new}} = (3491 \times 3491) - (3431 \times 3431) = -(3431 - 3491) \times 3491 = -40 \times 3491 = -139640
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m    \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Therefore, the area of the square decreases by \(\boxed{139640}\) square units.<|im_end|>
