[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To determine the greatest possible value of \( x \) such that the least common multiple (LCM) of \( x \), 10, and 14 is 70, we start by finding the prime factorizations of each number:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 10 = 2 \times 5, \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 14 = 2 \times 7, \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 70 = 2 \times 5 \times 7. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The LCM of three numbers \( x \), 10, and 14 is the product of the highest powers of all prime factors that appear in the factorizations of \( x \), 10, and 14. Therefore, we need the LCM of \( x \), \( 2 \), \( 5 \), and \( 7 \) to be 70. This means we need to express 70 as a product of these prime factors to its maximum power.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The prime factorization of 70 is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 70 = 2 \times 5 \times 7. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The highest powers of each prime factor in the prime factorization of 70 are \( 2^1 \), \( 5^1 \), and \( 7^1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Thus, the LCM of \( x \), 10, and 14 is \( x \) times \( 2 \), \( 5 \), and \( 7 \). Therefore, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ x = 70 \times 2 = 140. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To verify, we check the least common multiple of 140, 10, and 14:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The prime factorization of 140 is \( 2^2 \times 5 \times 7 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The prime factorization of 10 is \( 2 \times 5 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The prime factorization of 14 is \( 2 \times 7 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Thus, the LCM is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 2^2 \times 5 \times 7 = 140. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This confirms that the LCM is indeed 70, and the greatest possible value of \( x \) is \( 140 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Therefore, the greatest possible value of \( x \) is \(\boxed{140}\).<|im_end|>
