[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To find the common ratio of the infinite geometric series \(\frac{-3}{5} - \frac{5}{3} - \frac{125}{27} - \dots\), we first identify the first term \(a\) and the common ratio \(r\).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The first term \(a\) is \(\frac{-3}{5}\).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The common ratio \(r\) is the ratio of the second term to the first term, which is \(\frac{-\frac{5}{3}}{\frac{-3}{5}} = \frac{-5}{3} \cdot \frac{5}{-3} = \frac{25}{9}\).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To verify, we can also check the next term to ensure the common ratio is consistent.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The second term is \(-\frac{5}{3}\), and the third term is \(-\frac{125}{27}\). The common ratio is \(\frac{-\frac{125}{27}}{-\frac{5}{3}} = \frac{125}{27} \cdot \frac{3}{5} = \frac{125 \cdot 3}{27 \cdot 5} = \frac{125}{45} = \frac{25}{9}\).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Since the common ratio is consistent, we have \(a = \frac{-3}{5}\) and \(r = \frac{25}{9}\). Therefore, the common ratio of the series is \(\boxed{\frac{25}{9}}\).<|im_end|>
