[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To solve the equation \(2x^2 + 24x - 60 = x(x + 13)\), we start by expanding and simplifying the right side of the equation:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[2x^2 + 24x - 60 = x^2 + 13x.\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Next, we move all terms to one side of the equation to set it to zero:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[2x^2 + 24x - 60 - x^2 - 13x = 0.\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Combining like terms gives:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[x^2 + 11x - 60 = 0.\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This is a standard quadratic equation of the form \(ax^2 + bx + c = 0\), where \(a = 1\), \(b = 11\), and \(c = -60\). We can solve this quadratic equation using the quadratic formula \(x = \frac{-b \pm \sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\). Plugging in the values for \(a\), \(b\), and \(c\), we get:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[x = \frac{-11 \pm \sqrt{11^2 - 4 \cdot 1 \cdot (-60)}}{2 \cdot 1} = \frac{-11 \pm \sqrt{121 + 240}}{2} = \frac{-11 \pm \sqrt{361}}{2} = \frac{-11 \pm 19}{2}.\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This gives us two potential solutions:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[x = \frac{-11 + 19}{2} = \frac{8}{2} = 4,\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[x = \frac{-11 - 19}{2} = \frac{-30}{2} = -15.\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Since we are looking for the smallest possible value of \(x\), we discard \(x = -15\) and keep \(x = 4\).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Thus, the smallest possible value of \(x\) is \(\boxed{4}\).<|im_end|>
