[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To find the sum of all the positive factors of \(36\), we start by determining its prime factorization. The number \(36\) can be written as:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[36 = 6^2\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This is because \(36 = (2 \cdot 3)^2 = 2^2 \cdot 3^2\).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The formula to find the sum of the factors of a number \(n = p_1^{e_1} \cdot p_2^{e_2} \cdots p_k^{e_k}\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[(1 + p_1 + p_1^2 + \cdots + p_1^{e_1})(1 + p_2 + p_2^2 + \cdots + p_2^{e_2}) \cdots (1 + p_k + p_k^2 + \cdots + p_k^{e_k})\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m For \(36 = 2^2 \cdot 3^2\), the sum of the factors is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[(1 + 2 + 2^2)(1 + 3 + 3^2) = (1 + 2 + 4)(1 + 3 + 9) = 7 \cdot 13 = 91\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Thus, the sum of all the positive factors of \(36\) is \(\boxed{91}\).<|im_end|>
