[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To determine the fewest number of 3-inch by 4-inch tiles required to cover a rectangular region that is 2 feet by 5 feet, we need to follow these steps:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 1. Convert the dimensions of the region from feet to inches. Since 1 foot = 12 inches, the region is 24 inches by 60 inches.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 2. Calculate the area of the region and the area of one tile.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 3. Determine how many tiles are needed by dividing the area of the region by the area of one tile and rounding up to the nearest whole number if necessary.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 4. Account for any leftover space that is not completely covered with tiles.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m First, the area of the region is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 24 \text{ inches} \times 60 \text{ inches} = 1440 \text{ square inches} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The area of one tile is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 3 \text{ inches} \times 4 \text{ inches} = 12 \text{ square inches} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The number of tiles needed is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \left\lceil \frac{1440}{12} \right\rceil = \left\lceil 120 \right\rceil = 120 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m So, the fewest number of tiles required to completely cover the rectangular region is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \boxed{120} \]<|im_end|>
