[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To determine how many different values the greatest common divisor (gcd) of \( n + 5 \) and \( n + 11 \) can attain, we start by letting \( \gcd(n+5, n+11) = d \). This means that \( d \) divides both \( n + 5 \) and \( n + 11 \). Therefore, \( d \) must also divide any integer linear combination of \( n + 5 \) and \( n + 11 \). Specifically, \( d \) must divide:
