[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To solve the equation \((x - 5)^2 = 9\), we first take the square root of both sides. This gives us two different equations:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[x - 5 = 3 \quad \text{or} \quad x - 5 = -3.\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m We solve each equation separately.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m For the first equation \(x - 5 = 3\):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[x = 3 + 5 = 8.\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m For the second equation \(x - 5 = -3\):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[x = -3 + 5 = 2.\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m So, the roots of the equation are \(x = 8\) and \(x = 2\).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To find the sum of the roots, we add the two roots together:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[8 + 2 = 10.\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Therefore, the sum of the roots of the equation \((x - 5)^2 = 9\) is \(\boxed{10}\).<|im_end|>
