[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Steve has an isosceles triangle with base 8 inches and height 10 inches. He wants to cut it into eight pieces that have equal areas, as shown below. To the nearest hundredth of an inch what is the number of inches in the greatest perimeter among the eight pieces? [asy]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m size(150);
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m defaultpen(linewidth(0.7));
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m draw((0,0)--(8,0));
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m for(int i = 0; i < 9; ++i){
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m draw((4,10)--(i,0));
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m }
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m draw((0,-0.5)--(8,-0.5),Bars(5));
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m label("$8''$",(0,-0.5)--(8,-0.5),S);
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m [/asy]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m First, let's determine the area of the original isosceles triangle using the given base length and height:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \text{Area} = \frac{1}{2} \times \text{base} \times \text{height} = \frac{1}{2} \times 8 \times 10 = 40 \text{ square inches} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Next, we divide this triangle into eight equal pieces by drawing lines from the midpoint of the base to each of the three vertices along the same height. Each piece will be congruent and has an area of:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \text{Area of each piece} = \frac{40}{8} = 5 \text{ square inches} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To find the dimensions of each piece, we can use similar triangles. The height from the base to the highest vertex along each piece will be half of the height of the original triangle:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \text{Height of each piece} = \frac{10}{2} = 5 \text{ inches} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The base and legs of each piece must also be equal by similarity of triangles. Let's determine these dimensions:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \text{Length of each piece along the base} = \frac{8}{2} = 4 \text{ inches} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m For each piece, we can use the Pythagorean theorem to find the legs:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \text{Height}^2 + \text{Base/2} = \text{Length}^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 5^2 + 4 \times 5 = \text{Length}^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 25 + 20 = \text{Length}^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 45 = \text{Length}^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \text{Length} = \sqrt{45} \approx 6.70 \text{ inches} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The two remaining pieces can be calculated similarly:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \text{Height} \times 4 \times 2 = \text{Length}^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 5 \times 4 \times 2 = \text{Length}^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 40 = \text{Length}^2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \text{Length} = \sqrt{40} \approx 6.32 \text{ inches} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Finally, the greatest perimeter of these $8$ pieces would be the sum of their straightline lengths, which would be:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 8 \times (\text{Base} + 2 \times \text{Length}) = 8 \times (8 + 2 \times 6.70) = 8 \times 17.40 = 139.20 \text{ inches} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Thus, the greatest perimeter among the eight pieces (rounded to the nearest hundredth) is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \boxed{139.20} \]<|im_end|>
