[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m we can substitute \( y = 1 \) to get:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ f(x \cdot 1) = f(x) f(1) \implies f(x) = f(x) f(1). \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This implies that:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ f(1) = 1. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Next, we substitute \( x = -1 \) to find a value for \( f(-1) \):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ f(-x) = f(-1) f(x). \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This tells us that for any \( x \neq 0 \), \( f(-x) \) and \( f(x) \) are related, specifically that the function has a periodic behavior with respect to the reciprocal of \( x \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Now, we use the original equation again, substituting \( y = 0 \):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ f(x \cdot 0) = f(x) f(0). \]
