[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To determine the remainder when the 100th term of the Lucas sequence is divided by 8, we first need to understand the pattern and the periodicity of the sequence. The Lucas sequence is defined as follows:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ L_1 = 1, \quad L_2 = 3, \quad \text{and} \quad L_n = L_{n-1} + L_{n-2} \text{ for } n \geq 3. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m We can compute the first few terms to identify any patterns:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ L_3 = L_2 + L_1 = 3 + 1 = 4, \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ L_4 = L_3 + L_2 = 4 + 3 = 7, \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ L_5 = L_4 + L_3 = 7 + 4 = 11, \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ L_6 = L_5 + L_4 = 11 + 7 = 18, \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ L_7 = L_6 + L_5 = 18 + 11 = 29. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Next, we observe the sequence modulo 8:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ L_1 \equiv 1 \pmod{8}, \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ L_2 \equiv 3 \pmod{8}, \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ L_3 \equiv 4 \pmod{8}, \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ L_4 \equiv 7 \pmod{8}, \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ L_5 \equiv 11 \equiv 3 \pmod{8}, \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ L_6 \equiv 18 \equiv 2 \pmod{8}, \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ L_7 \equiv 29 \equiv 5 \pmod{8}. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m We notice that the sequence reaches a repeating cycle of 3 terms modulo 8 after the fourth term: \(4, 7, 3\). Therefore, we need to determine how many complete cycles occur in the first 99 terms and the effect of the remaining term.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The number of terms in the first 99 terms is 99/3 + 1 = 34 full cycles plus 1 additional term. Therefore, there are 34 full cycles plus 34 terms that do not complete a full cycle. Since 34 terms do not complete a full cycle modulo 8, they do not contribute to the remainder when divided by 8.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Thus, we only need to consider the 34 remaining terms of these cycles. Let's compute the sum of these 34 terms:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 4 + 7 + 3 = 14 \equiv 6 \pmod{8}. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The 100th term is \( L_{99} \), which is the same as \( L_4 \) because \( L_4 \equiv 7 \pmod{8} \). Therefore, \( L_{99} \equiv 7 \pmod{8} \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The remainder when the 100th term of the sequence is divided by 8 is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \boxed{7}. \]<|im_end|>
