[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To compute \( i^{600} + i^{599} + \cdots + i + 1 \), where \( i \) is the imaginary unit with \( i^2 = -1 \), we observe the powers of \( i \):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ i^1 = i \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ i^2 = -1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ i^3 = -i \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ i^4 = 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ i^5 = i \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ i^6 = -1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m and so on. The powers of \( i \) repeat every 4 cycles: \( 1, i, -1, -i \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m We need to determine the sum of the sequence \( i, i^2, i^3, \ldots, i^600 \). First, we determine the position of each power in its cycle. The position of \( i^k \) in the sequence of first 600 powers (with the first power being \( 1 \)) is given by the formula:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \text{Position} = 600k + 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m where \( k \) ranges from 0 to 100 (since 600/4 = 150, and considering the full cycle from 0 to 399).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The number of complete cycles of 4 powers in the first 600 terms is 150, which corresponds to the term at position \( 600 \mod 4 = 0 \). In this case, \( i^0 = 1 \).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The sum of the sequence \( i, i^2, i^3, \ldots, i^6 = 1+1-i-i^2-1+i = -i \). Therefore, the sum of the first complete cycles plus the remaining terms is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 1 + 1 - 1 - i - (-i) - i + i = 1 - i + i + i - i - i = 1 - i + i = 1 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m because the imaginary parts cancel out.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Thus, the sum \( i^{600} + i^{599} + \cdots + i + 1 \) simplifies to:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 1 + 1 = 2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
