[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To solve this problem, we first need to calculate the volume of the original box and the volume of the cube that is removed. Then, we can find the percent of the original volume that is removed.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The volume \( V_{\text{original}} \) of the original box is given by:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ V_{\text{original}} = 15 \text{ cm} \times 10 \text{ cm} \times 8 \text{ cm} = 1200 \text{ cm}^3. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The volume \( V_{\text{cube}} \) of the cube that is removed is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ V_{\text{cube}} = 3 \text{ cm} \times 3 \text{ cm} \times 3 \text{ cm} = 27 \text{ cm}^3. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The volume of the part of the box that is not removed is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ V_{\text{unremoved}} = V_{\text{original}} - V_{\text{cube}} = 1200 \text{ cm}^3 - 27 \text{ cm}^3 = 1173 \text{ cm}^3. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The percentage of the original volume that is removed is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \left( \frac{V_{\text{cube}}}{V_{\text{original}}} \right) \times 100 = \left( \frac{27}{1200} \right) \times 100 = 2.25\%. \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Thus, the percent of the original volume that is removed is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \boxed{2.25}. \]<|im_end|>
