[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To find the modular inverse of \(11\) modulo \(1000\), we need to find an integer \(x\) such that:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[11x \equiv 1 \pmod{1000}\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This means that there exists some integer \(k\) such that:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[11x = 1000k + 1\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m or equivalently,
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[11x - 1000k = 1\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This is a linear Diophantine equation, and we can solve it using the Extended Euclidean Algorithm. First, we apply the Euclidean Algorithm to find the greatest common divisor (gcd) of \(11\) and \(1000\):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[1000 = 11 \cdot 90 + 10\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[11 = 10 \cdot 1 + 1\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[10 = 1 \cdot 10 + 0\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The gcd is the last non-zero remainder, which is \(1\). Since the gcd is \(1\), \(11\) and \(1000\) are coprime, and an inverse exists. We now use the Extended Euclidean Algorithm to express \(1\) as a combination of \(11\) and \(1000\):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Starting from the Euclidean algorithm:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[1000 = 11 \cdot 90 + 10\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[11 = 10 \cdot 1 + 1\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[10 = 10 \cdot 1 + 0\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m We can backtrack to express \(1\) as a combination of \(11\) and \(1000\):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[1 = 1000 - 11 \cdot 90\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This equation tells us that:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[1 = -11 \cdot 90 + 10 \cdot 1000\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Thus, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[-11 \cdot 90 \equiv 1 \pmod{1000}\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m This means that \(x = -90\) is a solution to the equation \(11x \equiv 1 \pmod{1000}\). Since we want the answer to be in the range from \(0\) to \(999\) (inclusive), we convert \(-90\) to a positive equivalent by adding \(1000\):
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[-90 + 1000 = 910\]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Therefore, the modular inverse of \(11\) modulo \(1000\) is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[\boxed{910}\]<|im_end|>
