[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Suppose 6 wallops = 5 ballops. Also suppose 3 ballops = 11 fallops. How many wallops are equivalent to 110 fallops?
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Please reason step by step, and put your final answer within \boxed{{}}.<|im_end|>
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m <|im_start|>assistant
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To solve the problem, we need to establish a relationship between the wallops, ballops, and fallops. We are given two conversion percentages:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 1. 6 wallops = 5 ballops
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 2. 3 ballops = 11 fallops
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m First, we need to express these conversion rates in terms of wallops. From the first given conversion rate, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \frac{6 \text{ wallops}}{5 \text{ ballops}} = \frac{6 \text{ wallops}}{5 \text{ ballops}} \times \frac{5 \text{ wallops}}{3 \text{ ballops}} = \frac{30}{15} \text{ wallops per ballop} = 2 \text{ wallops per ballop} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m So, 1 ballop equals \( \frac{5}{3} \) wallops.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Next, from the second given conversion rate, we have:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \frac{3 \text{ ballops}}{11 \text{ fallops}} = \frac{3 \text{ ballops}}{11 \text{ fallops}} \times \frac{11 \text{ fallops}}{6 \text{ ballops}} = \frac{33}{66} \text{ fallops per ballop} = \frac{1}{2} \text{ fallops per ballop} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m So, 1 fallop equals \( \frac{6}{3} \) ballops, or 2 ballops.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Now, we need to find out how many wallops are equivalent to 110 fallops. We use the relationship between wallops and ballops:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 110 \text{ fallops} = x \times 2 \text{ wallops per ballop} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 110 \text{ fallops} = x \times 2 \text{ wallops per ballop} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ 55 = x \times 2 \text{ ballops per wallop} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ x = \frac{55}{2} \text{ ballops per wallop} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ x = 27.5 \text{ ballops per wallop} \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m So, \( x = 27.5 \). Therefore, 110 fallops are equivalent to 27.5 wallops.
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The answer is:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \boxed{27.5} \]<|im_end|>
