[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m To solve the inequality \(x^2 \cos \theta - x(1 - x) + (1 - x)^2 \sin \theta > 0\) for all \(0 \le x \le 1\), we first simplify the given expression. The inequality can be rewritten as:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ x^2 \cos \theta - x + x^2 + 1 - 2x + x^2 \sin \theta > 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ x^2 (\cos \theta + 1 - 2) + (\sin \theta - 1)x + 1 > 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ x^2 (\cos \theta + 1 - 2) + (\sin \theta - 1)x + 1 > 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m For this quadratic inequality to hold for all \(0 \le x \le 1\), the quadratic function \(f(x) = x^2 (\cos \theta + 1 - 2) + (\sin \theta - 1)x + 1\) must be greater than zero for all \(0 \le x \le 1\). This means that the quadratic function must be positive at the boundaries of the interval and the vertex of the parabola must also be positive (as it needs to be non-negative for all \(x\) in the interval).
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m First, we check the value of \(f(x)\) at the vertex, which is at \(x = \frac{-(\sin \theta - 1)}{2(\cos \theta + 1 - 2)} = \frac{\sin \theta - 1}{-2(\cos \theta + 1 - 2)} = \frac{\sin \theta - 1}{-2(\cos \theta + 2 - 1)} = \frac{\sin \theta - 1}{-2(\cos \theta + 2) + 2}\). For \(f(x)\) to be positive at this point, we need:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \cos \theta + 2 \neq 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \cos \theta \neq -2 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m Next, we need \(f(x)\) to be positive for \(0 \le x \le 1\). This means:
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \frac{\sin \theta - 1}{-2(\cos \theta + 2)} > 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m and
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m \[ \frac{\sin \theta - 1}{1} > 0 \]
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m 
[36m(main_task pid=3409)[0m The denominator \(-2(\cos \theta + 2)\) is always negative and unequal to 0 for all \(0 \le \cos \theta \le 1\). For the fraction to be positive, the numerator must be positive and the denominator to be negative. Here, the numerator \(\sin \theta - 1\) is positive for \(0 \le \sin \theta \le 1\). The only region in the interval \(0 \le x \le 1\) where this happens is \(\sin \theta \in [0, 1]\). For the fraction to be positive, \(\cos \theta\) must also be positive in this region, so:
